# pixel_tune - tweak registry
#
# One line = one tweak. The separator is a vertical bar. THE DESCRIPTION IS
# THE LAST FIELD, so it may contain anything except a vertical bar, a quote
# and a backslash (the JSON for the WebUI is emitted without escaping - a fork
# per description would cost 7.7 ms).
#
#   id|cat|mech|target|type|risk|flags|name|description
#
#   id      [a-z0-9._], must stay unique even after dots become underscores
#   cat     io vm sched cpu gpu net batt zram ui power disp exp
#   mech    sysfs | proc | prop | settings | cmd | bootcfg | ro
#   target  a path (may contain a glob), a property name, or namespace/key
#   type    bool | int:MIN:MAX | enum:a,b,c | enumf:@self | enumf:/path | ro
#   risk    0 = no risk, 1 = low, 2 = medium, 3 = high
#   flags   boot,reapply,unverified,adv  (empty = none)
#
#   boot        does not survive a reboot -> service.sh deploys it via tweak apply-boot
#   reapply     gets overwritten by applying a profile -> tweak reapply at the end of profile_apply
#   unverified  the effect on THIS build is not documented
#   adv         for advanced users, the WebUI should hide it behind a switch
#
# mech=bootcfg writes KEY=VALUE into a config file in STATE_DIR, not to the
# device. The target has the form file/KEY (the same as namespace/key for
# settings). It is used where writing at runtime is dangerous and the value is
# deployed by post-fs-data.sh at the next start - today the only case is the
# zram size.
#
# The stock value is NOT TAKEN from here - it is captured on first touch into
# backup/tweaks.stock. For glob targets a list of values is stored in path
# order.
#
# (The read-only entries were removed on 2026-08-13 - the registry now holds
# only what can actually be set.)

# ===========================================================================
# I/O - block layer and UFS
# ===========================================================================
io.read_ahead_kb|io|sysfs|/sys/block/sd[a-d]/queue/read_ahead_kb|int:0:8192|1|boot,reapply|Disk read-ahead (KB)|How much data the kernel reads ahead when a file is being read sequentially. A higher value speeds up large sequential reads (game launches, installs, copying), but with random access it wastes memory and power. Stock is 128 KB on sda and 1024 KB on sdb through sdd, which is why the stock value is stored as a list of four values.
io.nr_requests|io|sysfs|/sys/block/sd[a-d]/queue/nr_requests|int:4:1024|1|boot|Request queue depth|How many I/O requests may wait in the scheduler queue at once. More means better throughput during batched writes, but a longer wait for whatever you are actually looking at. The kernel may reject a value that is too high based on the UFS controller tag count - the write then fails and is logged.
io.rq_affinity|io|sysfs|/sys/block/sd[a-d]/queue/rq_affinity|int:0:2|1|boot|Where an I/O request completes|0 = any core handles the completion, 1 = a core from the same group, 2 = exactly the core that issued the request. Two reduces data bouncing between clusters and shortens latency, at the cost of occasionally waking a strong core just for a completion.
io.iostats|io|sysfs|/sys/block/sd[a-d]/queue/iostats|bool|0|boot|I/O statistics|Accounting of statistics for every request - what iostat and dumpsys diskstats read. Turning it off saves a small overhead on writes but makes it impossible to find out what is actually loading the disk. The gain is a few percent of I/O overhead, which is imperceptible in overall performance.
io.scheduler|io|sysfs|/sys/block/sd[a-d]/queue/scheduler|enumf:@self|2|boot|I/O scheduler|Determines the order in which requests are sent to the UFS. The active mq-deadline favours reads over writes, kyber targets low latency under load, bfq divides the disk fairly between apps (at the cost of higher CPU overhead) and none leaves ordering entirely to the controller. The change takes effect immediately, but a bad choice can noticeably lengthen response times during background writes.
io.ufs_clkgate|io|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/13200000.ufs/clkgate_enable|bool|2|boot|UFS clock gating|Automatic shutdown of the UFS controller clocks when nothing is being read or written. On (stock) saves battery while idle, off removes a few tens of microseconds of latency on the first access after an idle period. Turning it off measurably increases standby power draw and the benefit is on the edge of observability.

# ===========================================================================
# VM - memory management
# ===========================================================================
vm.dirty_expire_centisecs|mem|proc|/proc/sys/vm/dirty_expire_centisecs|int:100:60000|1|boot|How long data may wait to be written|The time in hundredths of a second after which changed data in the cache is considered old and must be written to disk. Stock is 3000, i.e. 30 seconds. A higher value merges more writes together and saves both the UFS and the battery, but on a sudden shutdown or crash you lose more unwritten data.
vm.dirty_writeback_centisecs|mem|proc|/proc/sys/vm/dirty_writeback_centisecs|int:100:60000|1|boot|How often the writeback thread wakes|The interval in hundredths of a second at which the thread flushing changed data from the cache to disk wakes up. Stock is 500, i.e. 5 seconds. A longer interval means fewer CPU wakeups while idle, but burstier writes that may collide with whatever you are doing.
vm.min_free_kbytes|mem|proc|/proc/sys/vm/min_free_kbytes|int:13693:82158|3|boot|Untouchable memory reserve|How much memory the kernel keeps aside for emergency allocations that must not fail. Stock is 27386 kB. Raising it reduces hitches during fast allocation but takes away from the cache and triggers background app killing sooner. Too low a value leads to freezes under pressure, which is why the range is hard-limited to half up to triple the stock value.
vm.watermark_scale_factor|mem|proc|/proc/sys/vm/watermark_scale_factor|int:10:1000|2|boot|Reclaim headroom|Determines how early before memory runs out kswapd wakes up and starts reclaiming. Stock is 200. A higher value reclaims ahead of time, so the system freezes less when switching apps, but works more in the background and pushes more into zram.
vm.compaction_proactiveness|mem|proc|/proc/sys/vm/compaction_proactiveness|int:0:100|1|boot|Proactive memory defragmentation|How aggressively the kernel continuously assembles free memory into contiguous blocks. Stock is 0, i.e. off, and that is sensible on a phone: compaction costs CPU time and wakes cores in the background. Only turn it on when apps report that they cannot get contiguous memory.
vm.stat_interval|mem|proc|/proc/sys/vm/stat_interval|int:1:120|0|boot|Memory statistics collection interval|How often, in seconds, the kernel updates its memory usage counters. Stock is 1 second. Extending it to 10 s saves several wakeups per second, which is measurable on battery while idle, and the only cost is coarser resolution of the numbers in vmstat.

# ===========================================================================
# MEMORY - MGLRU (multi-generational page management)
# ===========================================================================
mem.lru_gen_min_ttl|mem|sysfs|/sys/kernel/mm/lru_gen/min_ttl_ms|int:0:5000|2|boot|Working set protection (ms)|Multi-generational page management is enabled on this kernel. This value says how old the work still has to be for the kernel to refuse pushing it into zram. Stock is 0, i.e. no protection. The kernel documentation recommends 1000, which practically removes stalls when switching between apps. The cost is a higher chance that a background app gets killed when memory genuinely runs short; with eight gigabytes of memory and 3.7 GiB of zram that risk is small. Measured stock: 0.

# ===========================================================================
# CPU scheduler
# ===========================================================================
sched.pelt_multiplier|cpu|proc|/proc/sys/kernel/sched_pelt_multiplier|enum:1,2,4|2|boot|Scheduler reaction speed (PELT)|The multiplier for how fast the scheduler tracks thread load. 1 = the stock period of 32 ms, 2 = 16 ms, 4 = 8 ms. A higher value means the governor notices a rise in load sooner and raises the frequency faster - snappier touch response and smoother scrolling. The cost is more pointless jumps to high frequencies, i.e. higher power draw and more heat. It is an official knob from Google, not a hack, and it is the most interesting item in the whole registry.

# ===========================================================================
# CPU - clusters, uclamp, thermal throttling
#
# On this A16 build scaling_max_freq has permissions 664 system:system and
# writes HOLD (holds-test on policy0/4/8, the cap held for 40 s and was
# restored) - the earlier claim about 0444 permissions no longer applies, see
# cpu.max_freq_* below. The node is owned by the power HAL though, which drops
# the cap on the LAUNCH hint, hence reapply; a cleaner lever on the clock is
# the sched_pixel governor and its limit_frequency (section below). Cooling
# devices, in contrast, are rewritten by the thermal HAL roughly every 7 s and
# stay ro. The second working lever is uclamp in /dev/cpuctl - and that one
# belongs to the profiles, see cpu.uclamp_topapp below.
#
# Measured core capacities: Little 182, Big 725, Prime 1024 (the uclamp scale
# is 0-100 out of 1024). From that: uclamp.max up to 17.8 = the task stays on
# the small cores, up to 70.8 = it never reaches for Prime. On the big cores
# roughly frequency = (uclamp / 725) * 2367 MHz.
# ===========================================================================
cpu.max_freq_little|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy0/scaling_max_freq|int:324000:1704000|2|boot,reapply,adv|Small core frequency cap (kHz)|The small A510 cores can do 324000 to 1704000 kHz. On this A16 build scaling_max_freq has permissions 664 system:system and writes really do hold (verified by a holds-test on policy0 and policy8, the cap held for 40 s and was restored) - the earlier claim about 0444 permissions no longer applies. The node is owned by the power HAL though, which restores it uncapped on the LAUNCH hint, hence the reapply flag. A more precise and cleaner lever on the clock is the sched_pixel governor and its limit_frequency, see sched.limit_freq_little. Measured stock: 1704000.
cpu.max_freq_big|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy4/scaling_max_freq|int:402000:2367000|2|boot,reapply,adv|Big core frequency cap (kHz)|The big A715 cores range from 402000 to 2367000 kHz. On this A16 build scaling_max_freq can be written and holds (permissions 664 system:system, verified by a holds-test on policy4, the cap held for 40 s and was restored) - the earlier claim about 0444 read-only no longer applies. The node is owned by the power HAL, which drops the cap on the LAUNCH hint, hence reapply. A cleaner alternative is the sched_pixel governor limit_frequency, see sched.limit_freq_mid. Measured stock: 2367000.
cpu.max_freq_prime|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy8/scaling_max_freq|int:500000:2914000|2|boot,reapply,adv|Prime core frequency cap (kHz)|The single X3 core ranges from 500000 to 2914000 kHz and is by far the thirstiest. On this A16 build scaling_max_freq has permissions 664 system:system and writes hold (verified by a holds-test on policy8, the cap held for 40 s and was restored) - the earlier claim that the write does not go through even as root does not apply on this build. The node is owned by the power HAL though, which restores it to maximum on the LAUNCH hint, hence reapply. A more precise and cleaner replacement is the sched_pixel governor limit_frequency, see sched.limit_freq_big. Measured stock: 2914000.
cpu.uclamp_camera_min|cpu|sysfs|/dev/cpuctl/camera-daemon/cpu.uclamp.min|int:0:100|2|boot,reapply,adv|Camera performance floor (0-100)|Raises the minimum required utilisation of camera processes, so the frequency comes up sooner and both the preview and the launch feel snappier; stock is 0. A value above 17.8 means the task no longer fits on the small cores and the scheduler places it straight onto the big ones. The cost is higher power draw and heat whenever the camera runs, and earlier thermal throttling during longer recordings.
cpu.uclamp_camera_max|cpu|sysfs|/dev/cpuctl/camera-daemon/cpu.uclamp.max|int:0:100|2|boot,reapply,adv|Camera performance cap (0-100)|Caps the utilisation of camera processes, so the governor reaches for lower frequencies and the phone runs cooler and leaner during long recordings; stock is max, i.e. no limit. A value up to 17.8 keeps the task on the small cores, up to 70.8 the Prime core is never touched. Cutting it too far shows up as a stuttering preview, slow camera startup or dropped video frames.
cpu.uclamp_nnapi_min|cpu|sysfs|/dev/cpuctl/nnapi-hal/cpu.uclamp.min|int:0:100|2|boot,reapply,adv|Machine learning performance floor (0-100)|Applies to the NNAPI HAL, i.e. voice transcription, recognition in photos and similar work; stock is 1, which is only a symbolic floor. Raising it above 17.8 moves this work from the small cores to the big ones, so it finishes sooner, but it is paid for in power and background heat. It has no effect on the smoothness of everyday use.
cpu.uclamp_nnapi_max|cpu|sysfs|/dev/cpuctl/nnapi-hal/cpu.uclamp.max|int:0:100|2|boot,reapply,adv|Machine learning performance cap (0-100)|Caps the performance the machine learning HAL may reach; stock is max. Lowering it below 70.8 keeps it off the Prime core and noticeably cuts the heat during batch photo processing in the background, at the cost of waiting longer for the result. It is one of the few groups no profile touches, so no tweak fights anyone over the node here.

# ===========================================================================
# VENDOR SCHEDULER - /proc/vendor_sched
#
# A second, independent uclamp interface alongside /dev/cpuctl. The scale is
# 0-1024, NOT 0-100. Values from both sources are aggregated and the stricter
# one applies, so a setting in /dev/cpuctl above the vendor value has no
# effect at all.
#
# KEY CONSEQUENCE: groups/bg/uclamp_max is stock 130, i.e. 12.7 percent. The
# threshold below which a task stays on the small cores is 182 out of 1024.
# Google therefore keeps the background off the big cores out of the box, and
# the UCLAMP_BG_MAX keys in the powersave, game and night profiles are
# consequently without effect.
# ===========================================================================
vsched.dvfs_headroom|cpu|proc|/proc/vendor_sched/dvfs_headroom|int:1024:1536|1|boot,reapply|Frequency overshoot (DVFS headroom)|How much the governor adds on top of the frequency that utilisation says would be enough. Stock is 1100, which means 7.4 percent higher than needed; during an app start the power HAL raises it to 1280 for five seconds. Lowering it to 1024 means picking exactly the frequency needed, i.e. measurably less heat and power for the same work, at the cost of a slightly slower reaction to a sudden load spike. It is a cleaner lever than uclamp.max, because it does not change where tasks are placed between clusters. Measured stock: 1100 (a per-CPU vector of nine values; writing a single number copies it to all cores).
vsched.util_threshold|cpu|proc|/proc/vendor_sched/util_threshold|int:1024:4096|2|boot,adv|Threshold for moving to a stronger cluster|The utilisation at which the scheduler stops considering a task manageable on the current cluster. Stock is a per-CPU vector: 2048 for the four small cores and 1280 for the big ones and Prime. A higher number keeps work on the small cores for longer and saves power, a lower one sends it up sooner and speeds things up. The value is given either as a single number or as a space-separated vector. Measured stock: 2048 2048 2048 2048 1280 1280 1280 1280 1280.
vsched.tapered_headroom|cpu|proc|/proc/vendor_sched/tapered_dvfs_headroom_enable|bool|1|boot,adv|Tapering headroom|When enabled, the frequency overshoot shrinks towards the upper frequencies, so the most expensive operating points are not engaged needlessly. Stock is 0, i.e. off, and the power HAL holds it at zero during app starts. Enabling it is interesting precisely for a power saving profile. The effect on this build is not documented. Measured stock: 0.
vsched.bg_uclamp_max|cpu|proc|/proc/vendor_sched/groups/bg/uclamp_max|int:0:1024|2|boot,reapply,adv|Background performance cap (0-1024)|The real lever on background processes; the one in /dev/cpuctl is overridden by this one, because the stricter value applies. Stock is 130, which is below the small core threshold, so the background cannot reach the big cores out of the box. Lowering it below 130 further limits how quickly background work finishes; during an app start the power HAL raises it to 512 for five seconds anyway. It mainly makes sense in the night profile, when no apps are being launched. Measured stock: 130.
vsched.ta_uclamp_min|cpu|proc|/proc/vendor_sched/groups/ta/uclamp_min|int:0:1024|2|boot,reapply,adv|Foreground performance floor (0-1024)|The vendor floor for the app on screen; stock is 1, i.e. practically nothing, and the power HAL raises it to 765 on app start and on display wake. A permanent increase gives snappier response at the cost of power, but it works against the thermal loop, because 765 out of 1024 is far above the level the system itself holds under load. A sensible range for a permanent setting is up to 260. Measured stock: 1.
vsched.fg_uclamp_min|cpu|proc|/proc/vendor_sched/groups/fg/uclamp_min|int:0:1024|2|boot,reapply,adv|Performance floor for visible processes (0-1024)|Applies to visible but inactive processes. Stock is 0, the power HAL raises it to 159 on app start. Raising it helps where an app helper process wakes slowly, but for most everyday use it only adds power draw. Measured stock: 0.
vsched.ta_prefer_idle|cpu|proc|/proc/vendor_sched/groups/ta/prefer_idle|bool|1|boot,reapply|Look for an idle core for the foreground|When enabled, the scheduler prefers a core that is currently doing nothing for the app on screen, instead of adding it on top of running work. The result is a shorter wait to be scheduled and smoother scrolling, at the cost of waking another core and therefore higher power draw. Stock is off and the power HAL turns it on for five seconds during an app start. Measured stock: false.
vsched.ta_prefer_high_cap|cpu|proc|/proc/vendor_sched/groups/ta/prefer_high_cap|bool|2|boot,reapply,adv|Prefer strong cores for the foreground|Sends the app on screen straight to the big cores without having to fill the small ones first. It is a blunter version of what uclamp.min does in the performance profile, and like it, raises both power draw and heat. The effect on this build is not documented. Measured stock: false.
vsched.reduce_prefer_idle|cpu|proc|/proc/vendor_sched/reduce_prefer_idle|bool|1|boot,adv|Globally reduce idle core hunting|The opposite of the previous item, for the whole system. When enabled, the scheduler looks for a completely idle core less often and thereby saves wakeups. On this build it is enabled out of the box (true), so that saving is already accounted for; the power HAL, in contrast, temporarily disables it during app starts and display changes so that as many cores as possible wake up. Disabling it permanently would add wakeups and power draw, so leaving it at true is the economical setting. Measured stock: true.
vsched.uclamp_max_filter|cpu|proc|/proc/vendor_sched/uclamp_max_filter_enable|bool|2|boot,adv|Filtering of uclamp caps|Decides whether the scheduler ignores the utilisation cap of short tasks so as not to hold them back. Stock is 1, i.e. enabled, and the power HAL disables it during app starts. Disabling it means caps set by a profile apply more strictly to short tasks as well. The effect on this build is not documented. Measured stock: 1.
vsched.nnapi_uclamp_min|cpu|proc|/proc/vendor_sched/groups/nnapi/uclamp_min|int:0:1024|1|boot,reapply,adv|Machine learning floor (0-1024)|The vendor counterpart of cpu.uclamp_nnapi_min, but on the 0-1024 scale and with precedence. Stock is 225. Lowering it cuts heat during batch photo processing in the background, raising it shortens the wait for voice transcription. Measured stock: 225.

# ===========================================================================
# sched_pixel GOVERNOR - TUNABLES
#
# The sched_pixel governor has its own limit_frequency alongside
# scaling_max_freq, and Google power HAL writes into it. On this A16 build
# BOTH caps are writable and hold (see cpu.max_freq_* above), but
# limit_frequency is cleaner: it does not change node permissions, does not
# pretend to be a HW cap, and the governor works with it directly. The probe
# confirmed writes on policy0/4/8.
# ===========================================================================
sched.limit_freq_little|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy0/sched_pixel/limit_frequency|int:324000:1704000|2|boot,reapply|Small core frequency cap (kHz)|The second, working clock cap alongside scaling_max_freq. Stock is 1328000 and the power HAL switches between 1328000 and 1098000 based on utilisation measured by the CPU performance counters. Lowering it trims the peak on the small cores, which are the busiest of all three clusters under sustained load. Values outside the list of supported frequencies are rounded or rejected by the kernel. Measured stock: 1328000.
sched.limit_freq_mid|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy4/sched_pixel/limit_frequency|int:402000:2367000|2|boot,reapply|Big core frequency cap (kHz)|The same for the big A715 cores. Stock is 1836000, which is interesting in itself: it lies below the hardware maximum of 2367000, so the big cores do not run at full clock even without thermal throttling. Lowering it to 1328000 or 1221000 noticeably cuts the heat without touching the steady state the system holds under load around 910 MHz. Measured stock: 1836000.
sched.limit_freq_big|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy8/sched_pixel/limit_frequency|int:500000:2914000|2|boot,reapply|Prime core frequency cap (kHz)|The same for the single X3 core, which is by far the thirstiest and the main source of the thermal peak. Stock is 2363000 and the power HAL also knows 1852000 and 1298000. This is the single most effective value for reducing peak heating, because Prime settles around 1164 MHz under sustained load anyway. Measured stock: 2363000.
sched.down_rate_little|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy0/sched_pixel/down_rate_limit_us|int:500:50000|1|boot,reapply|How fast the small core clock falls (us)|The shortest time between two frequency reductions. Stock is 500, i.e. 0.5 ms - the ramp down is fast out of the box. A longer time holds the frequency up even after the work ends, which helps with stuttery scrolling but wastes power; shorter is practically impossible. Measured stock: 500.
sched.down_rate_mid|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy4/sched_pixel/down_rate_limit_us|int:500:50000|1|boot,reapply|How fast the big core clock falls (us)|Stock is 500, i.e. 0.5 ms, the same fast ramp down as on the small cores. Extending it holds the big cores up for longer, which only makes sense in games; there is nowhere left to shorten it. Measured stock: 500.
sched.down_rate_big|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy8/sched_pixel/down_rate_limit_us|int:500:50000|1|boot,reapply|How fast the Prime clock falls (us)|Stock is 500. On the most expensive core a fast ramp down is the most valuable, because every extra millisecond at a high clock translates directly into surface temperature - and out of the box it is already set aggressively. Measured stock: 500.
sched.spc_threshold_big|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy8/sched_pixel/spc_threshold|int:0:100|2|boot,unverified,adv|Performance counter threshold for Prime|The sched_pixel governor can tell that a task is waiting on memory rather than computing, in which case raising the clock makes no sense. This threshold decides when it behaves that way. Stock is 50, the power HAL also knows 22 and 20. A lower number means stricter judgement, i.e. fewer pointless jumps to a high clock. The effect on this build is not documented. Measured stock: 50.
sched.lcpi_threshold_big|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpufreq/policy8/sched_pixel/lcpi_threshold|int:0:16|2|boot,unverified,adv|Cycles per instruction threshold for Prime|A companion to the previous item. Determines the cycles-per-instruction value above which the governor concludes that a task is memory bound and raising the clock will not help it. Stock is 0. It is a very fine setting and there is no point moving it without measurements. The effect on this build is not documented. Measured stock: 0.

# ===========================================================================
# TEMPERATURE - runtime levers that SPEC.md does not know about
#
# Verified from the source: thermal_info_config_akita.json gives the sensors
# VIRTUAL-SKIN-CPU-MID and VIRTUAL-SKIN-CPU-HIGH a game profile, which has
# Disabled: true on all three CPU cooling devices. Setting the thermal profile
# to game therefore demonstrably DISABLES CPU throttling from those two
# sensors. user_vote_bypass only exists on thermal-cpufreq-0/1/2 (confirmed by
# the probe).
# ===========================================================================
thermal.cdev_bypass_little|cpu|sysfs|/dev/thermal/cdev-by-name/thermal-cpufreq-0/user_vote_bypass|bool|3|adv,unverified|Ignore thermal votes on the small cores|The power HAL knows this node as CPU_LITTLE_TSKIN_BYPASS, and the name suggests that a one makes the cooling device stop listening to the thermal HAL requests. If that held, it would be the only way to remove throttling derived from surface temperature at runtime without touching the vendor partition. The risk is high and appropriate: the phone really will get hot and nothing will stop it. Never set it permanently, nor in a profile without a thermal safety net of its own. Measured stock: 0.
thermal.cdev_bypass_mid|cpu|sysfs|/dev/thermal/cdev-by-name/thermal-cpufreq-1/user_vote_bypass|bool|3|adv,unverified|Ignore thermal votes on the big cores|The same for the big A715 cores, where the most performance is lost under sustained load; 910 MHz was measured, which is 38 percent of maximum. That is exactly why this is the most tempting and at the same time the most dangerous item in the whole registry. Only enable it briefly and only when you know what the surface temperature is. Measured stock: 0.
thermal.cdev_bypass_big|cpu|sysfs|/dev/thermal/cdev-by-name/thermal-cpufreq-2/user_vote_bypass|bool|3|adv,unverified|Ignore thermal votes on the Prime core|The same for the X3 core. Given that Prime is the largest single source of heat, the risk-to-reward ratio here is the worst of the three. Measured stock: 0.
thermal.tj_offset_big|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/100a0000.BIG/offset_enabled|bool|3|adv,unverified|Junction temperature offset for the big cores|Enables the offset by which the reported silicon temperature of the big cores is adjusted. The power HAL uses it in some scenarios. The probe found that this build does not expose the node holding the offset value (.../offset) at all - you can only turn the offset_enabled flag on or off, the size of the offset itself cannot be set. Because actual throttling on this phone is driven by surface temperature and not by junction temperature, the expected benefit is small while the risk is obvious: you are changing the chip protection logic. Included only for completeness. Measured stock: 0.

# ===========================================================================
# DEVFREQ - memory, shared cache, interconnect
#
# On Tensor this is a non-negligible part of both idle and load power draw,
# and none of the profiles touches it today. During an app start the power HAL
# pushes DSU to 1800000 and MIF to maximum, so it knows this matters for
# performance.
# ===========================================================================
devfreq.mif_target_load|mem|sysfs|/sys/class/devfreq/17000010.devfreq_mif/interactive/target_load|int:10:100|1|boot,reapply|Memory target load|Determines how busy the memory bus has to be before the governor raises its frequency. Stock is 20 40, i.e. a pair of thresholds. A higher first number keeps memory at a low frequency for longer and saves power, but a sudden increase in work waits longer. It is one of the few items that reduces power draw even with the display off. Measured stock: 20 40.
devfreq.mif_min_freq|mem|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/17000010.devfreq_mif/devfreq/17000010.devfreq_mif/min_freq|int:0:4206000|2|boot,reapply,adv|Memory lower frequency|The frequency below which the memory bus will not drop. Stock is 421000, so memory keeps a certain floor out of the box. Raising it shortens the delay when memory work suddenly arrives but holds power draw up even while idle; lowering it below stock reduces idle draw, if the bus allows it. The list of valid frequencies is in the available_frequencies file in the same directory. Measured stock: 421000.
devfreq.dsu_min_freq|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/17000090.devfreq_dsu/devfreq/17000090.devfreq_dsu/min_freq|int:0:2000000|2|boot,reapply,adv|Shared cache lower frequency|The DSU is the shared cache of all three clusters and its frequency is a surprisingly large power item on Tensor. Stock is 324000; the power HAL pulls it to 1800000 during app starts. Holding it up permanently speeds up communication between clusters, but it is one of the most expensive things you can enable. Measured stock: 324000.

# ===========================================================================
# IDLE POWER DRAW - when a cluster is disconnected from power
# ===========================================================================
cpupm.cl1_residency|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/cpupm/cpupm/cpd_cl1_target_residency|int:1000:2000000|2|boot,reapply|When to sleep the big cores (us)|How long the big core cluster has to be idle before disconnecting it from power pays off. Stock is 10000, i.e. 10 ms - the cluster is put to sleep very aggressively out of the box, so the idle saving is already extracted and lowering the value makes no sense. What is useful is rather RAISING it in games: a longer residency prevents repeated sleeping and waking between frames, which removes both latency and a small overhead. The price of a higher value is somewhat higher idle power draw. Measured stock: 10000.
cpupm.cl2_residency|cpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/cpupm/cpupm/cpd_cl2_target_residency|int:1000:2000000|2|boot,reapply|When to sleep the Prime core (us)|The same for the X3 core. Stock is 10000, i.e. 10 ms - Prime is put to sleep quickly out of the box and the idle saving is already accounted for. Because Prime wakes for short spikes and does nothing in between, it makes sense to RAISE the value in games so you do not pay for repeated wakeups between frames; lowering it below stock has no practical benefit. Measured stock: 10000.

# ===========================================================================
# ENERGY MODEL
# ===========================================================================
pixel_em.active_profile|exp|sysfs|/sys/kernel/pixel_em/active_profile|enumf:@self|3|unverified,adv|Energy model profile|The energy model is the table the scheduler uses to estimate how much each frequency on each core costs, and it decides where to send a task based on it. Google has the profiles default, cam1 and cam2 here. It is the closest thing to undervolting that exists on Tensor, because it does not change voltages but changes what the scheduler believes about power draw. A bad profile means systematically bad task placement, i.e. worse power draw and worse performance at the same time. The active_profile node is writable (unlike write_profile, which is write only), but the effect on this build is not documented. Measured stock: default.

# ===========================================================================
# CPUSET - CAREFUL, some of these are owned by the power HAL
# ===========================================================================
cpuset.background_cpus|cpu|sysfs|/dev/cpuset/background/cpus|enum:0,0-1,0-2,0-3|2|boot,reapply,adv|Which cores the background may run on|A hard core-mask restriction for background processes that works independently of uclamp. Stock is 0-3, i.e. all the small cores. Narrowing it to 0-1 reduces contention with the foreground over cache and is the only way to restrict the background even further once the vendor utilisation cap has done its part. The cost is a longer time for syncs and background jobs to finish. The foreground/cpus node, by contrast, is owned by the power HAL and gets overwritten, which is why it stays read only. Measured stock: 0-3.

# ===========================================================================
# Mali GPU (/sys/class/misc/mali0/device)
#
# Unlike the cooling devices, these nodes DO HOLD - verified by writing 649000
# to scaling_max_freq, which still applied after 12 s. It is therefore the only
# reliable lever on the GPU thermal budget. Frequencies (kHz) ascending:
#   150000 302000 337000 376000 419000 467000 521000 580000 649000 723000
#   807000 850000 890000
# Stock: max 890000, min 150000, power_policy adaptive.
# ===========================================================================
gpu.scaling_max_freq|gpu|sysfs|/sys/class/misc/mali0/device/scaling_max_freq|enum:150000,302000,337000,376000,419000,467000,521000,580000,649000,723000,807000,850000,890000|1|boot,reapply|GPU frequency cap (kHz)|The highest frequency the Mali may climb to; stock is 890000. Lowering it noticeably cuts heat and power draw in games and postpones the moment the thermal HAL starts throttling, at the cost of lower frames per second at the peak. The profile touches the same node through the GPU_MAX_FREQ key, so the tweak is deployed after it and has the final say.
gpu.scaling_min_freq|gpu|sysfs|/sys/class/misc/mali0/device/scaling_min_freq|enum:150000,302000,337000,376000,419000,467000,521000,580000,649000,723000,807000,850000,890000|2|boot,reapply|GPU lower frequency (kHz)|The frequency below which the GPU will not drop, even when it is drawing nothing; stock is 150000. Raising it shortens the delay of the first frame after an idle period, but holds the GPU needlessly high on the home screen and in the browser too, which is directly visible in power draw. It must stay below the cap, otherwise the write may not take.
gpu.hint_max_freq|gpu|sysfs|/sys/class/misc/mali0/device/hint_max_freq|enum:150000,302000,337000,376000,419000,467000,521000,580000,649000,723000,807000,850000,890000|1|boot,reapply,unverified,adv|Recommended GPU cap (kHz)|A second, softer cap alongside scaling_max_freq; stock is the same 890000. Exactly how the driver decides between the two is not documented on this build - lowering hint_max_freq alone may have no measurable effect while the hard cap is up. Included so it can be tried and measured; the real lever is gpu.scaling_max_freq.
gpu.power_policy|gpu|sysfs|/sys/class/misc/mali0/device/power_policy|enumf:@self|2|boot,reapply|GPU power policy|Determines how quickly the GPU is disconnected from power when it has nothing to do. adaptive (stock) switches individual shader cores on as needed, coarse_demand turns the whole GPU on and off at once, and always_on keeps it powered permanently. always_on removes a fraction of a millisecond from the first frame after an idle period, but raises idle power draw enough to be noticeable in a day of battery life.

# ===========================================================================
# GPU - nodes on the platform path (/sys/devices/platform/1f000000.mali)
# ===========================================================================
gpu.dvfs_period|gpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/1f000000.mali/dvfs_period|enum:10,20|1|boot,reapply|GPU frequency re-evaluation period (ms)|How often the driver decides which frequency to set the graphics to. During app starts and display changes the power HAL sets 10 ms for snappiness, otherwise 20 ms applies. A shorter period means a faster reaction to the start of rendering, a longer one less overhead and lower power draw. In games 10 makes sense, in the power saving and night profiles 20. Measured stock: 20.
gpu.hint_min_freq|gpu|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/1f000000.mali/hint_min_freq|enum:150000,302000,337000,376000,419000,467000,521000,580000,649000,723000,807000,850000,890000|2|boot,reapply,adv|Recommended GPU lower frequency (kHz)|A soft lower bound alongside the hard scaling_min_freq, used by the power HAL. Raising it shortens the delay of the first frame after an idle period, but holds the graphics needlessly high on the home screen too. Its relation to the hard bound is not documented on this build, just as with hint_max_freq. Measured stock: 150000.

# ===========================================================================
# Network
# ===========================================================================
net.tcp_slow_start_after_idle|net|proc|/proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_slow_start_after_idle|bool|0|boot|Do not slow TCP down after idle|After a few seconds of silence the kernel throws away the measured connection speed and starts ramping up from zero again. On both Wi-Fi and mobile networks that means every further image or request after a short pause starts slowly. Turning it off (0) keeps the already measured speed. It is the best tweak in the whole list: a noticeable speedup of ordinary browsing, no risk and zero cost on battery.
net.tcp_mtu_probing|net|proc|/proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_mtu_probing|int:0:2|1|boot|Finding the largest packet that gets through|When there is a link with a smaller MTU along the path and the signalling about it is being dropped, the connection stalls mid-transfer. A value of 1 enables probing only after a stall, 2 probes from the start. It mainly helps over VPNs and on some mobile networks; the cost is a few extra probe packets while establishing a connection.
net.tcp_fastopen|net|proc|/proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_fastopen|enum:0,1,2,3|1|boot|Fast connection setup (TFO)|Allows data to be sent already in the first packet, which removes one round trip. 1 = as a client only, 2 = as a server only, 3 = both. It saves tens of milliseconds on every new connection, but some outdated firewalls drop packets with TFO and the connection then takes longer to establish instead.
net.tcp_congestion|net|proc|/proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_congestion_control|enumf:/proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_available_congestion_control|1|boot|Congestion control algorithm|Decides how quickly TCP raises its speed and how it reacts to packet loss. The active one is bbr, which measures actual throughput and delay instead of counting losses and tends to be noticeably faster on wireless networks than cubic. The list of options is read straight from the kernel. Changing it only makes sense if a specific network does not agree with bbr.
net.default_qdisc|net|proc|/proc/sys/net/core/default_qdisc|enum:fq_codel,fq,pfifo_fast|2|boot|Default outgoing packet queue|Determines how outgoing packets are queued. fq_codel actively trims the queue and keeps latency low even during a full-speed download, fq is fair queueing by flow (what bbr expects), pfifo_fast is a plain queue with no cleverness. Note: the change only affects newly created interfaces, so in practice after toggling Wi-Fi off and on or after a reboot.
net.wifi_scan_always|net|settings|global/wifi_scan_always_enabled|bool|1||Scan for Wi-Fi even with Wi-Fi off|Android scans nearby networks for location purposes even when you have Wi-Fi turned off. Turning it off (0) saves regular radio wakeups and is noticeable in overnight power draw. The cost is slower and less accurate positioning indoors and worse behaviour of some location-dependent apps.
net.private_dns_mode|net|settings|global/private_dns_mode|enum:off,opportunistic,hostname|1||Private DNS|off = unencrypted DNS from your carrier, opportunistic = encrypted wherever the other side supports it, hostname = enforced encrypted DNS to a specific server (its address is configured separately in private_dns_specifier). Encryption costs one extra round trip on the first query, but hides which domains you are asking about. In hostname mode the network stops working if the server is unreachable.

# ===========================================================================
# Battery and charging
# ===========================================================================
batt.charge_stop_level|batt|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/google,charger/charge_stop_level|int:1:100|1|boot,reapply|Stop charging at (%)|Charging stops above this level. Keeping the battery around 80 % instead of 100 % markedly slows its ageing - this battery already has 602 cycles behind it. The cost is shorter runtime per charge. Note: the profile touches the same node, so the tweak is deployed after the profile is applied and has the final say.
batt.charge_start_level|batt|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/google,charger/charge_start_level|int:0:99|1|boot,reapply|Resume charging below (%)|Charging starts again below this level. Together with the stop level it forms a band the phone floats within when left permanently on the charger. A wider band (say 65 to 80) means fewer recharge cycles, a narrower one keeps the state of charge steadier but hovers around a single value more often.
batt.bd_trigger_temp|batt|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/google,charger/bd_trigger_temp|int:250:500|2|boot|Battery protection - trigger temperature (0.1 °C)|Google Battery Defender protection. When the battery is above this temperature for longer than bd_trigger_time while still on the charger, the system starts limiting charging. Stock is 350, i.e. 35.0 °C. Raising it postpones the protection, so the battery spends more time full and warm - which is exactly what destroys it fastest.
batt.bd_trigger_time|batt|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/google,charger/bd_trigger_time|int:600:604800|2|boot|Battery protection - how long to wait (s)|How long the conditions must hold before Battery Defender activates. Stock is 21600 s, i.e. 6 hours - exactly matching the overnight charging scenario. Extending it practically disables the protection and shortens the life of a cell that already has 602 cycles on it.
batt.bd_recharge_soc|batt|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/google,charger/bd_recharge_soc|int:0:100|2|boot|Battery protection - recharge from (%)|While Battery Defender is active, it lets the state of charge fall and only recharges from this level. Stock is 79 %. A lower value means fewer recharge cycles and gentler treatment of the cell, but you may find less charge in the morning than you expected.
batt.bd_resume_soc|batt|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/google,charger/bd_resume_soc|int:0:100|2|boot|Battery protection - end protection below (%)|Below this level Battery Defender switches off and charging returns to normal. Stock is 50 %. It is a safeguard against the phone staying stuck at half charge when you unplug and plug it back in. Lowering it makes no sense, raising it partially defeats the protection.
batt.bd_temp_enable|batt|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/google,charger/bd_temp_enable|bool|2|boot|Battery protection - enabled|The main switch of Google thermal battery protection. It is not a performance limit nor an annoyance - it is the only thing preventing the battery from spending whole nights charged to 100 % and warm. Turning it off (0) demonstrably shortens cell life, and this battery already has 602 cycles behind it. Only turn it off when you know exactly why, and preferably only temporarily.
batt.bd_temp_dry_run|batt|sysfs|/sys/devices/platform/google,charger/bd_temp_dry_run|bool|1|boot|Battery protection - dry run only|When set to 1, Battery Defender evaluates the conditions and reports to the log but does not intervene in charging. Useful when you want to find out whether and when the protection triggers for you at all, without disabling it outright. On its own it is not a performance tweak but a diagnostic.

# ===========================================================================
# zram
#
# zram is not touched at runtime: every change requires a swapoff, and that
# dumps all swapped-out data back into RAM (risk of OOM). The only settable
# item is the size, and that goes via bootcfg -> /data/adb/pixel_tune/zram.conf,
# which post-fs-data.sh reads at startup, when swap is empty.
# ===========================================================================
zram.disksize|mem|bootcfg|zram.conf/ZRAM_DISKSIZE|int:268435456:7939923968|2|adv|zram size in bytes (takes effect after a restart)|How much compressed swap the system reserves in RAM; stock is 3969961984 B, i.e. 3.70 GiB. It cannot be changed at runtime, because that would mean a swapoff, which with a full swap risks apps being killed for lack of memory - the value is therefore only stored in zram.conf and deployed by post-fs-data.sh at the next start. A larger zram keeps more apps in memory but takes away from free RAM and adds work for the compressor; a sensible range is roughly 2 to 6 GB.

# ===========================================================================
# UI and haptics
# ===========================================================================
ui.window_animation_scale|ui|settings|global/window_animation_scale|enum:0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1,1.5,2|0||Window animation speed|The multiplier on the length of animations when opening and closing windows. 0.5 means twice as fast, 0 disables animations entirely. A lower value feels snappier and saves a few milliseconds of GPU work per transition. Zero, however, exposes jumps in transitions and breaks timing in some apps, which is why 0.5 is the sensible limit.
ui.transition_animation_scale|ui|settings|global/transition_animation_scale|enum:0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1,1.5,2|0||Screen transition speed|The same as for window animations, but for transitions between activities. Shortening it to 0.5 is the most noticeable of the three scales for the impression of phone speed, because you see this transition every time an app opens. It does not increase actual performance, it only shortens the wait.
ui.animator_duration_scale|ui|settings|global/animator_duration_scale|enum:0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1,1.5,2|0||In-app animation speed|Affects animations that apps draw themselves - expanding lists, springy effects, loading indicators. Setting 0 causes the most trouble here: some apps wait for an animation to finish and without it behave strangely or freeze halfway. The recommended limit is 0.5.
ui.vib_context_enable|ui|prop|persist.vendor.vibrator.hal.context.enable|bool|0||Contextual haptics - enabled|The switch for Google logic that adjusts vibration strength to the situation (one strength in your hand, another on a table or in a pocket). Turning it off unifies the response to one fixed strength, which some people prefer. The property is read by the vibrator HAL at startup, so the change only fully takes effect after a reboot.
ui.vib_context_scale|ui|prop|persist.vendor.vibrator.hal.context.scale|int:0:100|1||Contextual haptics - intervention strength|How strongly the contextual logic intervenes in vibration strength. A lower number gives a gentler, quieter response and slightly lower vibration motor power draw, a higher one a more pronounced click while typing. It only fully takes effect after the vibrator HAL restarts, i.e. after a reboot.
ui.vib_context_cooldowntime|ui|prop|persist.vendor.vibrator.hal.context.cooldowntime|int:0:5000|1||Contextual haptics - delay between pulses (ms)|How long to wait after one vibration before the next contextual adjustment may apply. A shorter time makes the response denser and more continuous during fast typing, a longer one spares the motor and the battery and prevents individual clicks from smearing together. Takes effect after a reboot.
ui.vib_context_fade|ui|prop|persist.vendor.vibrator.hal.context.fade|bool|0||Contextual haptics - smooth fade out|Enables a smooth fade at the end of a vibration instead of a hard cut. On feels gentler and more expensive, off sharper and more precise. It has practically no effect on performance or battery, it is purely a matter of taste. Takes effect after a reboot.
ui.vib_context_settlingtime|ui|prop|persist.vendor.vibrator.hal.context.settlingtime|int:0:1000|1||Contextual haptics - settling time (ms)|How long the HAL waits before considering the device state (in hand, on a table) settled and recomputing the vibration strength. A shorter time reacts faster but the vibration strength then fluctuates with every movement; a longer one is more stable but delayed. Takes effect after a reboot.

# ===========================================================================
# Power management
# ===========================================================================
power.app_standby_enabled|batt|settings|global/app_standby_enabled|bool|1||App Standby|Android puts apps you have not used for a long time to sleep by itself and limits their background work and network access. Turning it off (0) lets all apps run freely - it can fix missed notifications from problematic apps, but it noticeably raises standby power draw. It is one of the main reasons a Pixel barely draws anything while idle.
power.adaptive_battery_management_enabled|batt|settings|global/adaptive_battery_management_enabled|bool|1||Adaptive Battery|Machine learning that predicts which apps you are about to use and cuts background work for the rest. Turning it off (0) improves notification delivery and removes unpredictable behaviour in apps you use only occasionally, but takes away from battery life.
power.phantom_procs|batt|settings|global/settings_enable_monitor_phantom_procs|enum:true,false|1||Kill background child processes|Since Android 12 the system counts the child processes an app spawns ("phantom processes") and kills the app once it goes over the limit, even when the app is holding a wake lock. On a phone that runs terminal tooling (Termux with an SSH tunnel, a bot, root scripts) this shows up as the whole app tree dying at random, taking the remote access with it. false disables the monitoring; the cost is that a runaway background process is no longer stopped for you. Verified on this device: the Termux tree was killed while several root shells were open, sshd kept the port open but stopped answering.

# ===========================================================================
# Display
# ===========================================================================
disp.screen_off_timeout|disp|settings|system/screen_off_timeout|enum:15000,30000,60000,120000,300000,600000,1800000|0||Screen timeout (ms)|How long the display stays on after the last touch. It is the single largest source of heat and drain on this phone: measured over 63 hours, the screen was on 41 % of the time and 94 % of the minutes with the battery above 40 C had the screen on. 1800000 is 30 minutes, which means the phone lies on the desk lit up for half an hour; 60000-120000 is the reasonable range. The same setting is in Settings > Display.

# ===========================================================================
# Experimental - the effect on THIS build is not documented
# ===========================================================================
exp.sf_early_phase_offset_ns|exp|prop|debug.sf.early_phase_offset_ns|int:0:16000000|2|boot,unverified,adv|SurfaceFlinger - early phase offset (ns)|Shifts the moment SurfaceFlinger wakes up to compose a frame in early phase mode. A smaller value may shave a little off the delay between touch and pixel, a larger one gives composition more time and reduces the risk of a dropped frame. The effect on this build is not documented and it only takes effect after SurfaceFlinger restarts, i.e. practically after a reboot.
exp.sf_early_app_phase_offset_ns|exp|prop|debug.sf.early_app_phase_offset_ns|int:0:16000000|2|boot,unverified,adv|SurfaceFlinger - early app phase offset (ns)|The same, but for the moment drawing wakes up inside the app. A smaller value shortens the path from touch to image, but at the same time gives the app less time to finish the frame, so stalls appear more easily. Not verified on this build.
exp.sf_early_gl_phase_offset_ns|exp|prop|debug.sf.early_gl_phase_offset_ns|int:0:16000000|2|boot,unverified,adv|SurfaceFlinger - GL phase offset (ns)|A variant of the previous one for the case where the hardware compositor cannot handle composition and it has to go through the GPU. That happens mainly with complex scenes with many layers. The effect on this build is not documented.
exp.sf_early_gl_app_phase_offset_ns|exp|prop|debug.sf.early_gl_app_phase_offset_ns|int:0:16000000|2|boot,unverified,adv|SurfaceFlinger - GL app phase offset (ns)|The app-side counterpart of the previous item, i.e. when drawing wakes up inside the app while composition runs through the GPU. The effect on this build is not documented.
exp.hwui_use_hint_manager|exp|prop|debug.hwui.use_hint_manager|bool|1|boot,unverified,adv|HWUI - report deadlines to the scheduler|Lets the drawing layer tell the system how much time it needs per frame so the governor raises the frequency in time. It sounds good and on some builds it really does remove stalls, but on this one the effect is not documented. Takes effect after a reboot.
exp.laptop_mode|exp|proc|/proc/sys/vm/laptop_mode|int:0:600|2|boot,unverified,adv|Laptop mode|Originally a mode for laptops with a spinning disk: it batches writes so the disk stays spun down as long as possible. On UFS there is nothing to spin up, so the expected benefit is zero to questionable, while burst writes that collide with whatever you are doing are a real risk. Included only so you can try it and measure it.
exp.thp_enabled|exp|sysfs|/sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled|enumf:@self|2|boot,unverified,adv|Transparent huge pages|always = the kernel merges memory into huge pages everywhere, madvise = only where an app asks for it, never = never. Huge pages reduce address translation overhead, but on a phone with fragmented memory and zram they more often hurt (expensive compaction, worse compression) than help. The effect on this build is unverified.
